well what a night joining us now is the  chairman of the conservative party  oliver dad and very good morning hello  good morning  uh let's start by talking about last  night if we may um an absolute dropping  wasn't it what went wrong  well voters in north shropshire were fed  up and they gave us a kicking  and i think they wanted to send us a  message and i want to say as chairman of  the conservative party we've heard that  loud and clear  we need to get on with delivering the  job and that's precisely what we're  doing for example with the booster  program to deal with its own macron  variant we've administered over 700 000  vaccines just yesterday i would though  say that these are quite unique  circumstances  and in a by-election like this you would  expect the opposition party of the  labour party to be surging they were  strong second and in fact they're  sinking but it wasn't normal by election  dynamics was it you talk about special  circumstances but really um you've  gifted this to the lib dems and you say  that they sent you a message the voters  there what is the message that you're  receiving and is it an endorsement do  you think of your leader well voters are  clearly fed up and they want us to get  on with the job and focus on the job and  our job as a government particularly at  a time of crisis like this is to make  sure we're focused on the core  priorities number one is to get on with  delivering the booster program that's  the way we stay open and we maintain  normal life during this very difficult  period the prime minister's put turbo  boosters under that and we've had over  700 000  boosters administered the primary on the  economy we've we've got some of the  lowest unemployment since before the  crisis but that's where our focus isn't  about the booster jab is it this is a  referendum on the prime minister  effectively is he still an electoral  asset for the conservatives yes i think  the prime minister is an electoral asset  for the conservative party and i'll tell  you why just take the three really big  calls we've faced get brexit done he's  delivered on it we've delivered the  fastest people would say they're not  scientists we've delivered the fastest  booster program  in the world twice over and we've seen  the economy through this terrible period  with unemployment lower than when we  entered so i think actually on the big  calls the prime minister has the vision  and the direction to get us through this  difficult period a lot of the red wall  tories they were going to be worried  aren't they and any tory indeed with a  slim majority they're going to be  worried about their seats you've got a  problem  well  we see results like this mid-term it's  it's the case it happens time and time  again you know between 1989 and 2014 we  didn't win a single by-election in  government we lost brexit to the liberal  democrats before the 2019 election we  won it back if the liberal democrats did  as well in by-elections uh in general  elections they don't buy elections  they've been in power for the past 50  years it is the case that people have  sent us a message we've heard that  message but i wouldn't extrapolate too  much through to the general so mumblings  about no confidence in the prime  minister are conversations being had  behind closed doors and letters being  written  i have no idea where the letters are are  being written but i have every  confidence the prime minister has the  leadership to see us through this period  and i think  he he has shown whether it's in relation  to getting brexit down or this latest  record-beating booster campaign that  he's got that drive and determination  again we'll come on to the booster  campaign i know you're clearly very keen  to talk about it more stories though  today uh in the day's papers your leader  himself the prime minister is accused of  attending a party at number 10 during  the first lockdown your comments on that  please well i understand that this was a  meeting that took place in the garden  actually it was the case at the time if  you're going to have a meeting you're  better off having it outside because of  the the fresh air and and ventilation so  will questions be asked will there be an  investigation into that well i've seen  the the account given by by downing  street that this there was a meeting  that took place and it took place  outside i think that was a perfectly  appropriate and unreasonable  there had been a press conference  previously  and it was perfectly reasonable to have  the meeting afterwards outside so will  simon case be looking into the details  of that one as well i don't think  there's a need for the cabinet secretary  to look into this one because the the  circumstance appear to be pretty clear  um cchq we understand again from the  papers that police are quizzing two  people um about that party that took  place there with sean bailey is it right  that police are quizzing two people that  were there at cchq and they're not  looking into what happened at the  various parties that are being alleged  at number 10.  well you wouldn't expect me as a cabinet  minister to comment on the operationally  independent metropolitan police but in  respect of the  completely unauthorized party that took  place  at conservative hq by the london mayoral  campaign it was appalling it was wrong  it should never have happened and the  people involved have been punished okay  let's uh let's move on another front  page that i don't think you'll like the  look of uh it's beginning to look a lot  like christmas terrible pun there  but the idea is that's the direction of  travel are we looking at more  restrictions ahead of christmas  well these are very difficult times and  we've seen with this surge of omicron  doubling almost every two days we're  having to take very difficult decisions  and i think anyone that that pretends  that this is simple or straightforward  is pretending however i do think right  now we have got the balance right the  key thing is the booster if you can can  you come back to the resolution this is  a very important this is a very  important point though about the booster  because it's only if people get that  booster that we can continue with this  kind of freedoms that we have now it  gives you 75  protection against it probably more  against hospitalization that is the  plank on which we're building our  strategy um wales is bringing in new  restrictions scotland has gone further  um why are we always a step behind and  even the queen has cancelled her  christmas parties shouldn't the  messaging be clearer  well the message is clear that people  need to be sensible and cautious but  because of the vaccine because of the  booster we are confident that we can  have restrictions as they stand at the  moment alongside the further mask  wearing and so on  it's because of the strength of that  booster and that's why it's essential  that we keep getting that booster into  people's arms and we've had record  success with that yesterday with over  700 000 administered i think that is the  correct and balanced approach all right  less than what what is targeted though  at a million you are asking people to  choose effectively though aren't you  between  the advice being given by the chief  medical officer professor chris witte  and the government very mixed messaging  there but it appears that those as  though people's trust is going with the  medical professionals we've seen the  cancellations we've seen a real drop off  in people using public transport uh  they're not trusting the government on  this  well chris whitty with whom you might  imagine i speak frequently prime  minister cabinet and i are all in  agreement and the single best thing we  can do in this situation is to get the  booster rolled out as rapidly as  possible we made that big call back in  the summer to buy the pfizer modern  another big call by the prime minister  that is very effective against this new  omicron variant  we're saying to everyone get your  booster if you get your booster we can  build back up that wall of defense and  continue with life as it was before just  one last question about uh chris whitty  reports in the papers that he's under  fire from tory mps for effectively  freelancing and advising people in a way  that doesn't accord with what the  government is saying is your party at  war with the cmo well i have a great  deal of respect for for chris whitty and  i think we should respect his integrity  and independence as an advisor of course  advisors adviser and ministers decide  and are accountable for the strategy of  off the back of that and i know that  these are difficult times and people  have what do you think  were you personally disappointed with  his intervention at the press conference  i think it's perfectly appropriate for  chris witte to advise the government  which is exactly what he has done but is  equally perfectly appropriate for the  government to make decisions off the  back of that and that's what we've done  with this strategy the core part of  which is the boost campaign i'm sorry to  keep on talking about it but it is so  essential that people get that boost one  other thing i want to talk to you about  before we get on to boosters a business  community crying out for support the  chancellor was swallowing around in  california what is going on is there any  more support for businesses well the  chancellor certainly wasn't spawning  around the chancellor was out in the  united states representing the united  kingdom meeting with leading businesses  in the united states he's also been in  constant contact for example with the  hospitality industry and the the prime  minister we have demonstrated time and  time again that we will stand behind  business when they need that support for  example when i was culture secretary i  remember when lockdown started i was  warned that we could lose a third of  football clubs two-thirds of our  cultural institutions thanks to the  record cultural recovery fund two  billion pounds we've preserved almost  all of them there's money there there's  money in relation to to business right  now i know you say that  forgive me for interrupting mr dowden um  i know you say he's meeting with  businesses in america it's very  important businesses say that they're  going to go to the wall over christmas  here they say where is he he should be  meeting with us he didn't show up to a  round table yesterday he also says that  existing support is enough but the  businesses say they won't survive  through christmas so what's going to  happen is is there any hope of more  support for them  well the chancellor is is returning to  the united kingdom to today i i  understand  if you look at the kind of support  that's available take the business rate  really through to spring that will mean  that businesses are paying a quarter of  the business rates they would normally  pay for that's why we've cut v80 and  kept vat cut at 12 and a half percent  there's also over a quarter of a billion  pounds still with local authorities that  could go to support businesses and i  know the chancellor is working with them  to get that money out so we have proven  that we'll put the resources in and we  will support those businesses and i know  the chancellor's committed to doing that  all right but no new money as far as you  know at the moment as you sit here  we have already put support in place of  course we watch this hour by hour  day by day and i know the chancellor is  very closely engaged but at the moment  we have appropriate supports in place  all right oliver dowden thank you very  much for coming in to see us at sky news  have a great christmas

